  "notes": "The lane's songwriter-artist, carried with the entry's explicit **Gate 1 caveat** — independent-label rather than platinum, \"included because the lane cannot be modelled without his songwriting grammar\". The drums take the first of the two options the entry gives: \"live kit (Chris Dave appears on his records) with displaced snares and ghost 16ths\", so the snare carries a 7–17 ms displacement and a ghost probability of 0.6 across six positions, with the percussion layer \"continuous\" at 3–6 hits a bar. The band is the stated 72–102. Harmony is the two families the entry writes out — IMaj7–vi7–ii7–V7 and IMaj7–IVMaj7, reduced to degrees with the sevenths moved into `extensions` — over \"8-bar sections with genuine harmonic motion\" and \"harmony written on keys, not looped\", which is why the harmonic rhythm keeps real movement rather than vamping. The load-bearing melodic claim is \"conversational songwriting — verses with long, prose-like lines at high syllabic density resolving to a short, simple, repeated hook\", which is density 5–10, a question-answer structure, `endVariation` and 4-or-8-bar phrases; the \"audience-participation vamp out\" is the long outro in the arrangement. Bass is \"walking with chromatic approach notes and real countermelody\". ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability and velocity and all melody/countermelody numbers; the research marks its tempo and harmonic detail as inference and [LOW-CONF].",
